{"prompt": "Paragraph 1\n\nYou probably learned in school that our solar system has nine planets, and Pluto is the last one. Then in 2006, Pluto lost its planet status and became a dwarf planet. Many of us assumed there was nothing big left in this story. But on October 27, 2026, news arrived that reopened it. Beyond Pluto\u2019s average distance, about 46 astronomical units away, a new dwarf planet candidate has been found, named 2026 ED138. And the most shocking part is that it was already out there, nobody had just seen it. So the question is, how did something this big stay hidden until now?\n\nParagraph 2\n\nThe answer lies with the Vera C. Rubin Observatory, which sits on Cerro Pachon in Chile. On the evening of October 13, scientist Matthew Holman was running a search code that matches billions of moving detections against each other. The code flagged something so bright and so far away that Holman could hardly believe it. His first move was to check whether he or the code had made a mistake. Then the team dug through the data and found 20 more observations, so the object was seen 30 times across 20 nights, from March 4 to July 11, 2026. Even more, older images traced its path back to 2016, giving more than 10 years of orbit. The reason is that it sat near a part of the Milky Way with millions of stars, and it got lost among them.\n\nParagraph 3\n\nNow let\u2019s see how big it is. Rubin\u2019s team estimates ED138 could be between 600 and 1,500 kilometers across. For comparison, Pluto\u2019s diameter is about 2,377 kilometers. So ED138 is smaller than Pluto, but it could be anywhere from a quarter to two thirds of its size. In distance, it is beyond Pluto\u2019s average: ED138 is currently about 46 astronomical units out, while Pluto\u2019s average is about 40. One astronomical unit is the distance from Earth to the Sun. At that distance, sunlight takes a little over 6 hours to reach it. Its orbit is tilted about 25 degrees from the plane of the solar system, which suggests Neptune\u2019s gravity may have pushed it out of place at some point. But remember, it is still just a candidate, not confirmed. Infrared observations are needed to settle it.\n\nParagraph 4\n\nIf ED138 is confirmed, it will be the sixth dwarf planet, joining Pluto, Ceres, Eris, Haumea and Makemake. A new name in a very small family. And Rubin has only just begun: its big survey started in June 2026, and the telescope will scan the entire southern sky again and again for 10 years. According to Holman, there could be more hiding in places older surveys left behind. Think about it, our own solar system may still hold worlds nobody has even seen. We kicked Pluto out of the planet club, and the universe answered by dropping another hidden sibling right in front of us. What do you think, should ED138 get dwarf planet status? Let us know in the comments.", "total": 300.0, "size": "16:9", "parts": [{"title": "Opening", "brief": "Opening (part 1 of 10 of the long video \"Paragraph 1\n\nYou probably learned in school that our solar system has nine planets, and Pluto is the last one. Then in 2006, Pluto lost its planet status and became a dwarf planet. Many of us assumed \"): continue the story without repeating earlier parts.", "seconds": 30.0, "spec": {"title": "The Pluto Mystery - Part 1: Reopening the Solar System", "duration": 30.0, "size": "16:9", "fps": 30, "background": [{"t": 0.0, "style": "ocean"}, {"t": 7.0, "style": "mono_dark", "fade": 0.6}, {"t": 15.0, "style": "neon_night", "fade": 0.6}, {"t": 22.5, "style": "sunset", "fade": 0.5}], "camera": [{"t": 0.0, "zoom": 1.0, "x": 960, "y": 540}, {"t": 6.8, "zoom": 1.08, "x": 1020, "y": 540}, {"t": 7.4, "zoom": 1.0, "x": 960, "y": 540}, {"t": 14.8, "zoom": 1.07, "x": 920, "y": 520}, {"t": 15.4, "zoom": 1.0, "x": 960, "y": 540}, {"t": 22.2, "zoom": 1.06, "x": 960, "y": 550}, {"t": 22.8, "zoom": 1.0, "x": 960, "y": 540}, {"t": 30.0, "zoom": 1.1, "x": 960, "y": 520}], "script": [{"t": 0.8, "text": "You pro